  ;; Agent.dispatch(fn, args, exec) is the one method on the reference types that
  ;; is a jolt SUPERSET rather than a match, so it is a unit row and not a corpus
  ;; one: jolt runs one serialized worker per agent, so the executor argument has
  ;; nothing to select and is accepted and ignored, where the JVM needs a real
  ;; Executor (given null it answers the agent and silently never runs the
  ;; action). jolt has no Agent/pooledExecutor to name, so no common row exists.
  {:suite "agent-interop" :label "dispatch enqueues the action and answers the agent" :expr "(let [ag (agent 1)] [(identical? ag (.dispatch ag + (seq [10 100]) nil)) (do (await ag) @ag)])" :expected "[true 111]"}
  {:suite "agent-interop" :label "dispatch with no args is a plain send" :expr "(let [ag (agent 1)] (.dispatch ag inc (seq []) nil) (await ag) @ag)" :expected "2"}
  ;; This is a unit row rather than a corpus one because it is deterministic on
  ;; jolt alone: jolt's await drains the queue AND clears running? before it
  ;; returns, so the count is exactly 0 after it, while the JVM's await can
  ;; return with its own wait action still queued and answer 1 under load. The
  ;; portable half — a fresh agent, never sent to — is a corpus row.
  {:suite "agent-interop" :label "getQueueCount counts the actions still pending" :expr "(let [ag (agent 0)] (await ag) (.getQueueCount ag))" :expected "0"}
