  ;; bytes-allocated reads the heap as it stands: live data AND garbage not yet
  ;; collected. Two readings around an allocation therefore do not order — a
  ;; collection landing between them can free more of the earlier rows' garbage
  ;; than the new data adds, and did on CI. What holds whatever the collector
  ;; does is the lower bound: the reading counts the data still referenced.
  {:suite "telemetry" :expr "(let [keep (into [] (map (fn [i] (str \"padpadpadpad\" i)) (range 100000))) b (jolt.host/bytes-allocated)] [(count keep) (> b (* 100000 16))])" :expected "[100000 true]"}
